Big-O Notation
1. What is Big-O?

Big-O notation describes the upper bound of an algorithm's time or space growth as input size n increases.

It is mainly used to describe the worst-case complexity of an algorithm.

Simple Meaning

Big-O tells us: "Algorithm maximum kitna grow kar sakta hai?"

For example:

T(n) = 3n² + 5n + 10

For large n, the dominant term is n².

Therefore:

T(n) = O(n²)
2. Why Do We Use Big-O?

Big-O helps us:

Compare algorithms
Predict performance for large inputs
Ignore machine-dependent execution time
Focus on growth rate
Analyze time and space requirements
3. Common Big-O Complexities
Complexity	Name	Example
O(1)	Constant	Access array element
O(log n)	Logarithmic	Binary Search
O(n)	Linear	Linear Search
O(n log n)	Linearithmic	Merge Sort
O(n²)	Quadratic	Bubble Sort
O(n³)	Cubic	Simple matrix operations
O(2ⁿ)	Exponential	Some recursive problems
O(n!)	Factorial	Brute-force permutations
4. Examples
Example 1: Constant
int x = arr[0];

Only one operation.

Time Complexity = O(1)
Example 2: Linear
for(int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    System.out.println(i);
}

Loop runs n times.

Time Complexity = O(n)
Example 3: Quadratic
for(int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    for(int j = 0; j < n; j++) {
        System.out.println(i + " " + j);
    }
}

Outer loop → n

Inner loop → n

Total:

n × n = n²


Time Complexity = O(n²)
Example 4: Logarithmic
n → n/2 → n/4 → n/8 → ... → 1

Number of steps:

log₂ n

Therefore:

O(log n)

Binary Search is a common example.

5. Rules for Big-O
Rule 1: Ignore Constants
T(n) = 5n

Remove constant 5.

O(n)
Rule 2: Keep Dominant Term
T(n) = n² + n + 10

Dominant term = n²

O(n²)
Rule 3: Sequential Operations
O(n) + O(n²)

Take the larger growth:

O(n²)
Rule 4: Nested Loops
for(i = 0; i < n; i++)
    for(j = 0; j < n; j++)

Multiply:

O(n × n) = O(n²)
6. Important Point

Big-O is an upper-bound notation mathematically.

It does not always mean exactly "worst case."

However, in algorithm discussions, Big-O is commonly used to express worst-case complexity.